====<span class="anchor" id="Confusion"></span> Confusion over the reason for a declined draft ====
{{shortcut|WP:AIWHYDECLINE}}
AI chatbots cannot read the decline notice left on the draft, and can only respond to whatever information regarding the declined draft that the user gives them. As a result, AI-generated questions regarding declined drafts will often express uncertainty or request clarification over the reason a draft was declined, even if the decline notice on the draft is very clear. They may even compose self-contradictory comments that simultaneously acknowledge the specific reason for the draft's decline, but go on to say that they are trying to understand whether that was ''really'' the reason, or whether it was declined for some completely unrelated reason.

{{blockquote|{{highlight|The latest decline says the draft needs multiple published secondary sources}} that provide significant coverage, are reliable, and are independent. {{highlight|I understand the concern}}... I am trying to understand {{highlight|whether the problem is mainly that these sources are not strong enough for WP:BIO / WP:NARTIST, or whether the draft structure and tone are still the main issue.}}|2=from [[Special:Diff/1356136271|this post]] at the [[WP:AFCHD|Articles for Creation Helpdesk]]}}

How could the "draft structure and tone" still be the "main issue" when it has just been specifically acknowledged what the issue is?

{{blockquote|I am requesting guidance on Draft:Alexis Marcou after repeated AfC declines... I am trying to understand {{highlight|whether the issue is mainly source quality, article tone, or both}} before making any further changes.|From [[Wikipedia:WikiProject Articles for creation/Help desk/Archives/2026 May 15#21:10, 15 May 2026 review of submission by Mike1582|this archive]] of the [[WP:AFCHD|Articles for creation helpdesk]] concerning a draft that was declined for not meeting [[WP:NPERSON]] and reading like an advertisement}}

Note that the latest decline notice on that draft when this question was asked was for both notability and promotional tone; so the answer to this question is obviously 'both'.

{{blockquote|Is the primary issue related to notability, sourcing, tone, conflict of interest, or article structure?|From [[Wikipedia:WikiProject Articles for creation/Help desk/Archives/2026 May 13#13:28, 13 May 2026 review of submission by Revathyradhakrishnan2412|this archive]] of the [[WP:AFCHD|Articles for creation helpdesk]]}}

{{blockquote|In particular, I would like to understand: Whether the issue relates to notability, sourcing, or tone If any specific sections need revision or removal|From [[Wikipedia:WikiProject Articles for creation/Help desk/Archives/2026 April 18#17:38, 18 April 2026 review of submission by Imanmiraki|this archive]] of the [[WP:AFCHD|Articles for creation helpdesk]]}}

=== <span class="anchor" id="Complaints about speculation"></span> Complaints about accusers acting on speculation ===
{{shortcuts|WP:CAAAOS|WP:SPECULATIONABOUTAIUSE}}
{{tmbox|image=none|text=Words to watch: {{strong|''speculation/speculative/speculating'', ''stylistic indications'', ''unsubstantiated/unsupported/unwarranted''}} }}
When a user is confronted for allegedly pasting content from a large language model, they will likely dismiss these concerns as unsubstantiated speculation based on tone or [[WP:STYLEDOESN'TMEANAI|writing style]] rather than [[WP:NOPROOFOFAI|hard evidence]] of LLM use. Most if not all users who do so also tell those who confront them to just [[WP:ORCC|point out what needs to be improved]] and [[WP:LETSFOCUSON|focus on that instead of calling them out]].

'''Examples'''
{{blockquote|{{fmbox|image=none|text=
