In many cases, editors have responded to accusations of AI use by denying that they have used AI to write content ([[WP:AINOVIOLATION|or that Wikipedia prohibits doing so]]) and calling for discussions to instead be [[WP:LETSJUSTFIXIT|focused solely on the improvement of affected articles]]. Such editors often [[WP:ORCC|ask accusers to help them]] by [[WP:WHERESTHEAI|pointing out which contributions]] have [[WP:FIXEDAITONE|tone issues]] or other problems.

When told that the general ''pattern'' of their editing has been [[WP:LLMDISRUPT|disruptive]], they often [[WP:IDHT|dismiss the accusation]] as being merely [[WP:CAAAOS|speculative]], unfounded, or [[WP:AIWL|having no basis in policy]], and accuse their critics of acting in a manner that they believe is [[WP:Incivility|uncivil]], hostile, dismissive, unprofessional, counterproductive, or an affront to Wikipedia's collaborative nature. Persistence from others is interpreted as aggression; if challenged sufficiently they may state their intent to withdraw from the discussion as it has become "emotionally charged". Since the editor did not write this comment themselves, they will often continue the discussion or return to it shortly afterwards.

AI will seldom confirm that it's being used without the prior consent of the user, so if the response is vague, sidesteps the question or [[WP:AIACCUSERISMEAN|considers it to be a personal attack]], this may be a sign of AI use.

