== Miscellaneous ==

=== Canned unblock requests ===
{{further|Wikipedia:Identifying LLM unblock requests}}
{{for|non-AI-specific guidance about this|Wikipedia:Guide to appealing blocks}}
When a user is blocked, they might ask an AI chatbot to write an unblock request for them. Many AI-generated unblock requests tend to go something like this:
{{xt2|Dear Wikipedia Administrators,<br>
I respectfully request that the block on my account be lifted. I acknowledge that my recent behavior has been in violation of Wikipedia's standards. My intention was to edit constructively, and I sincerely apologize if my behavior has appeared disruptive. I have since carefully read and reviewed all of Wikipedia's policies and guidelines, and I now understand the importance of adhering to them when editing. Moving forward, I am committed to ensuring that all of my future edits are constructive. If there are any specific concerns that need further clarification, feel free to ask me about them, and I will be happy to provide the answers you may need. I look forward to collaborating in a constructive manner. Thank you for your time and consideration.}}

In these requests, blocked users often try to [[WP:AIASSURE|assure administrators of their good faith and (future) adherence to policies]], express their willingness to [[WP:Communication is required|communicate]], and [[WP:ORCC|offer to receive constructive criticism]] for their edits.

=== Canned ANI reports ===
{{further|Wikipedia:The LLM-written ANI report}}

=== Sealioning ===
{{shortcut|WP:AISEALION}}
[[Sealioning]] is a disruptive practice where someone relentlessly demands users to provide evidence to back up the statements they make. When targets express frustration over the sealioner's behavor, the sealioner criticizes the targets for responding negatively to them, even though they were [[just asking questions]].

Several AI users have resorted to sealioning when confronted about their AI use. They write long comments that sometimes present arguments as [[WP:AILIST|entries in bulleted lists]] or [[WP:AISECTION|separated into multiple sections]]. They often [[WP:WHERESTHEAI|ask which specific passages]] have caused suspicion and [[WP:ORCC|need to be fixed]], and accuse confronters of [[WP:CAAAOS|acting on unsubstantiated speculation]] and [[WP:AIACCUSERISMEAN|being uncivil]].

=== Wikilawyering ===
{{shortcut|WP:AIWL}}
{{for|non-AI-specific guidance about this|Wikipedia:Wikilawyering}}
Wikilawyering is a disruptive practice where someone selectively cites or interprets policies, guidelines, or perceived precedent as justification for their conduct, even if their interpretations go against the purpose of the policies or guidelines they mention. In many cases, users have [[#Emphasis on adherence|emphasized their content's compliance]] (or overlooked their non-compliance) with certain policies and guidelines. This is especially the case for users of AI chatbots, which often generate text that affirms what the user may want others to believe, even if the points they present don't actually hold up against Wikipedia's policies and guidelines.

When an article is tagged as possibly containing AI-generated content, a user might try to defend it by asking accusers to [[WP:WHERESTHEAI|point to specific passages causing concern]] or reassuring them that the content they've contributed is "neutral", "verified" by citations to reliable sources, and comprises none of the items described in [[Template:AI-generated]], regardless of the amount of actual effort put in to ensure that such claims are true.

Common AI wikilawyering tropes include:
* Affirming that one's content [[WP:AIADHERENCE|adheres to Wikipedia's policies and guidelines]]. A few core content policies—such as ''[[WP:NPOV|Neutral point of view]]'', ''[[WP:V|Verifiability]]'', ''[[WP:RS|Reliable sources]]'', and ''[[WP:NOR|No original research]]''—are often [[WP:ITEMIZE|itemized]] by LLM users.
* Emphasizing that [[WP:NOLLM|Wikipedia's LLM guideline]] allows AI to be used for [[WP:AICOPYEDIT|basic copyediting]] or [[WP:LLMTRANSLATE|translation]].
* Outright [[WP:AIPROHIBDENIAL|denying that Wikipedia prohibits using AI]] to write or rewrite content at all.
* Claiming that accusations of AI use must be backed by [[WP:NOPROOFOFAI|hard evidence]] asides from [[WP:STYLISTIC|stylistic indicators]]. In some cases, [[WP:AIDETECTIVE]] has been cited.

== Ineffective indicators ==
False accusations of AI use can [[WP:BITE|drive away new editors]] and foster an atmosphere of suspicion. Before claiming AI was used, consider whether the [[Dunning–Kruger effect]] or [[confirmation bias]] may be clouding your judgement. Detecting LLM texts on the basis of style alone is not as easy as it seems, see [[WP:AIDETECTIVE]]. Here are some somewhat commonly-used indicators that are ineffective in LLM detection—and may even indicate the opposite.

* '''Letter-like writing (in isolation)'''{{snd}}Although many talk page messages written with [[salutation]]s, [[valediction]]s, [[WP:SUBJECTLINE|subject line]]s, and other formalities after 2023 tend to appear AI-generated, letters and emails have conventionally been written in such ways ''long'' before modern LLMs existed. Human editors (particularly newer editors) may format their talk page comments similarly for various reasons, such as being more accustomed to formal communication, posting as part of a school assignment that requires this tone, or simply mistaking the talk page for email. Other tells, such as [[WP:AILIST|vertical lists]], [[WP:AISIGNS#Phrasal templates and placeholder text|placeholder]]s, or [[WP:AISIGNS#Abrupt cut offs|abrupt cutoffs]], are stronger.
{{shortcut|WP:AIREVIEW}}
* {{anchor|Review}}'''Canned requests for source assessment (in isolation)''' – Although a few LLM users who have their drafts declined may go to the [[WP:WikiProject Articles for creation/Help desk|AfC help desk]] to ask for an editor (in some cases, an "experienced" or "uninvolved" one) to advise them as to which sources (out of the ones they're trying to use) help establish the [[WP:GNG|notability]] of a given topic,{{efn|A few examples of such requests can be found via {{plain link|url=https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=%22advise+which+of%22+%22if+any%2C+count+as%22+%22coverage+for%22&title=Special%3ASearch&profile=advanced&fulltext=1&ns4=1 this search link}}.}} such inquiries have also been made by users who ''don't'' use LLMs. In some cases, a user might be trying to figure out how certain cells on a particular [[WP:Source assessment|source assessment]] table should be filled.

